march is colorectal cancer awareness  month and rocky spoke to a southwest  medical doctor to learn more here's  their conversation  of course we've got a lot to talk about  i know you are a gastroenterologist  please define that for everybody what do  you do so i  am a stomach and colon doctor basically  i deal with intestinal diseases  like ulcers uh colon cancers  um you know anything that has to do with  the gi tract  march is colorectal cancer awareness  month so this is a great time to  educate everybody on the statistics  right  correct colorectal cancer is the third  most common diagnosed cancer  in the united states and 147 000 people  can be diagnosed with cancer in one year  and 50  53 000 people can die of colorectal  cancer  every year so it's definitely  a cancer that that can be prevented  and that's what we're here to do  absolutely prevention is key here so  what should people  look for you know when they're when  they're trying to self-diagnose  mainly people should look for  common symptoms would be bleeding so  blood in your stool  or blood in the toilet bowl change in  your bowel habits if they become thinner  or smaller  or you start having diarrhea abdominal  pain  weight loss that's unexplained so  colonoscopy is what we use  to prevent colon cancer and it's a  procedure that i do  that's done as an outpatient to look for  either polyps  which are lesions in the colon that can  turn into cancer if not removed  or actual colon cancer so if i see a  polyp i would remove it and  prevent that person from getting colon  cancer other screening tests would be  uh two stool tests that we have  currently one is the fit test  uh which is done at home and it looks  for blood in the stool microscopic blood  meaning blood that you might not be able  to  see um and so that's one of the tests  and then the most recent test that we  have is  called the cola guard test which is  actually a stool test that looks for  cancer or pre-cancerous cells  in the stool which can be seen  with large polyps or actual colon cancer  so if these tests are positive then you  would want to  proceed with the colonoscopy colon  cancer is very preventable  with colonoscopy or the other screening  modalities everyone over the age of  50 and actually recently has been  lowered to 45  up to 75 to 80 should be  screened regularly for colon cancer  because that's the only way we're going  to prevent it well how often should  people get a colonoscopy  um so it depends um upon their family  history  um and if they've had polyps in the past  um usually it's every 10 years for the  normal population  but can be up to five years if you have  a family history of colon cancer  or have a history of polyps southwest  medical has several locations across  southern nevada  for more information call 702-877-5199  or visit smalv.com  las vegas now
 

